From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:58:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: size the decoder's input blocks from the device's coded
 buffer size

The decoder asked the framework for input blocks of 16 MiB, and 64 MiB
above 1080p. The framework allocates every input block at that size and a
stream keeps several of them, so a 4K stream held about 0.9 GB of input
memory, while the component copies only the bytes a frame uses and the
device takes coded buffers of 8 MiB.

Read the size of the coded buffers the device negotiates from its input
queue when the codec is listed (a TRY_FMT with no size requested, which
the driver raises to the largest packet it takes), and make that the
default maximum input size, within 1 MiB and 16 MiB. A device that states
none gets 16 MiB.

The maximum input size is never below that size, whatever a client
states: a client states the largest sample of the clip it starts with
(a few tens of KB in CTS AdaptivePlaybackTest) and the stream carries
larger ones later. The old calculator raised the value for the same
reason. The floor reaches the setter through a read-only setting of the
component's own, since a setter cannot see the interface. The decoder
logs a device that needs more than the blocks it has.
---
