 common/include/v4l2_codec2/common/Common.h    |  4 ++
 components/DecodeInterface.cpp                | 61 ++++++++-----------
 .../v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h  | 20 ++++--
 v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp                          |  7 +++
 v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp                           | 24 ++++++++
 v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h    |  4 ++
 7 files changed, 99 insertions(+), 44 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index c2a99109d9d6663be009d810586dfe1d90b6613a..8c552cbfb5743f85cb65ba869a615fa93b2296ac 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -482,12 +482,23 @@ again with every queue operation.
 ### Input Blocks
 
 The device takes a coded buffer in a dma-buf at least as large as the buffer
-size it negotiated, and refuses a smaller one. The input blocks the framework
-allocates for the component are as large as the maximum input size and are
-queued as they are. A client of the block model chooses the size of its own
-blocks, and the component copies a block smaller than the maximum input size
-into a block of a pool it creates on first use, and queues that one. A secure
-block is never copied.
+size it negotiated, and refuses a smaller one. The component reads that size
+from the device when it lists the codec (the size the input queue reports for
+the codec before any is requested) and makes it the default maximum input
+size, within 1 MiB and 16 MiB; a device that states none gets 16 MiB. A client
+may state a larger maximum, but never a smaller one: the parameter is raised to
+the device's size, since a client states the largest sample of the clip it
+starts with and an adaptive stream carries larger ones later. The size comes
+from the device because the framework allocates every input block at the
+maximum input size and a stream keeps several of them: blocks of 64 MiB held
+about 0.9 GB for one 4K stream, while the component copies only the bytes a
+frame uses. The decoder logs a device that needs more than the block size.
+
+The input blocks the framework allocates for the component are as large as the
+maximum input size and are queued as they are. A client of the block model
+chooses the size of its own blocks, and the component copies a block smaller
+than the device's size into a block of a pool it creates on first use, and
+queues that one. A secure block is never copied.
 
 ### Picture Size
 
